# CP9 — fix the world, then re-measure.
#
# A86 gives `request_mandate_update` a way to succeed; A87 turns the agent ->
# runner dependency around; A88 makes a 10k OURS run survivable; then every
# number from CP8 is measured again over a batch where 17% of cases are no
# longer unwinnable by construction.

SPEC.md
PRIORS.md
settle/sim/world.py
settle/sim/generator.py
settle/schema/state.py
settle/runner/case_runner.py
settle/policy/grid.py
settle/agent/policy.py
settle/runner/arms/explore.py
settle/agent/estimator.py
tests/test_world.py
tests/test_policy.py
checkpoints/cp9.allowlist

# --- outside the stated allowlist, and why --------------------------------
#
# Part A as specified is inert without all five of these. Each is stated with
# what would have happened had it been left alone.
#
# streams.py — the §14.2 stream set is closed and `stream_value` raises on a
# name it does not know, so `mandate_revival_draw` cannot exist without a row
# here. The instruction names the stream; this is where streams live.
settle/sim/streams.py
#
# executor.py — a pending re-authorisation is world state, and RUN-9 forbids
# `settle/runner/` from importing `settle.sim`. The runner has to ask something
# at the world boundary, and §17 says the executor is that boundary. Two
# functions added, no behaviour changed.
settle/execute/executor.py
#
# legal.py — `dead_instrument` had no debit verb at all, so a revived mandate
# bought nothing: G3 would have stopped blocking a retry that `legal_actions`
# never offered. This is where A86's "RETRY is viable <=> mandate ACTIVE"
# derivation lives, next to A66's serve_notice rule.
settle/policy/legal.py
#
# taxonomy.py — FORBIDDEN_ACTIONS listed RETRY for `dead_instrument`
# unconditionally, which contradicts the derivation above. Narrowed to
# SWITCH_RAIL; the condition is a property of `mandate_state`, which a
# type-level table cannot express.
settle/diagnose/taxonomy.py
#
# params.py — G10's `class_retry_cap.dead_instrument` was 0. Correct while the
# class could never retry; a silent block on the one path it now has.
settle/policy/params.py
#
# tests/test_taxonomy.py — LEG-1 and LEG-2 encoded the unconditional ban. LEG-1
# is now parameterised over the three dead mandate states and gains the
# positive case; LEG-2 states the widening rather than waving it through.
tests/test_taxonomy.py
#
# tests/test_streams.py — STR-4 asserts the stream count. 10 -> 12.
tests/test_streams.py
